  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1151 PM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nA 596 dam ridge remains parked over the Desert Southwest, placing\nthe Mid-South in northwest flow aloft. Guidance continues to\nbring a shortwave out of the Rockies today, becoming embedded\nwithin the outer periphery of the ridge. This disturbance will\nincrease shower and thunderstorm chances within the Mississippi\nDelta. There is potential for associated convection to remain\nwest of the forecast area due to dry air aloft and lower\ndewpoints. If precipitation does occur in the Delta, amounts will\nbe negligible. As for afternoon highs, the NBM continues to show\na 7 degree spread amongst the 25th and 75th percentiles. This is\nlikely due to issues in modeling cloud cover associated with the\naforementioned precipitation. Regardless, afternoon highs will be\nmuch cooler than recent days as temperatures remain in the upper\n80s to low 90s. As for Tuesday, lingering showers and storms\ncannot be ruled out through mid-morning but the latter half of\nthe day should remain dry.\n\nAdditional disturbances will eject out of the Rockies midweek\ninto the weekend. This will keep daily rain chances in the\nforecast. The greatest rainfall potential will be Wednesday into\nThursday as a warm front lifts north and a cold front approaches\nfrom the Upper Mississippi Valley. Confidence in severe weather\npotential is low at this time, but given persistent northwest\nflow aloft and forecast SBCAPE in excess of 1500 J/kg a few\nstrong storms cannot be ruled out. Details will become clearer as\nwe move closer to midweek. Beyond Thursday's frontal passage,\nconditions will dry out for the upcoming weekend. Unfortunately,\nguidance brings hot and humid conditions back in the forecast\nwith heat indices reaching headline criteria by Saturday. Until\nthen, the probability of reaching 105 + heat index values is less\nthan 20 percent.\n\nANS",
